From 195739f45ab1bf5f61ce786212ce89e2e2cf1e64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:45:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Restore pre-run WebVOWL bytes when docs-widoco fails

docs-widoco snapshots the working-tree docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json as the
normalizer baseline, then rsync overwrites that same path with fresh WIDOCO
output. A failure between the two left raw generator bytes in the tree. The
next run adopted those raw bytes as its baseline, found the fresh raw output
semantically equal to them, restored them, and exited 0 -- a failed docs build
turning itself into a green build over un-normalized output.

The recipe now arms a shell trap on EXIT that puts the pre-run bytes back on
any non-zero exit, and disarms it once the normalizer has succeeded, past which
the file holds normalized output and the rest of the recipe is cleanup. The
restore is byte-exact and free, because the baseline copy the recipe already
takes is a copy of those bytes.

The working-tree baseline is kept deliberately: it is what makes repeated local
refreshes compare against the previous local run rather than against the last
commit. Sourcing the baseline from git HEAD instead was considered and rejected
for that reason (Brett, 2026-09-14). That rejected option was the Intended fix
stated in the tech-debt entry, so it is recorded as rejected in ADR-010 and in
the resolved entry rather than deleted, to avoid leaving the repository
teaching it.

Verified by reproduction, not by a clean run, since a clean run never enters
the failure path. With an injected normalizer abort the unfixed recipe leaves
raw bytes and the repaired re-run exits 0 over them (8219 lines changed against
the committed artifact); with the trap the failed run restores the pre-run
bytes and the repaired re-run is green on a clean tree. Both versions fail
identically at the break step, so breaking the build proves nothing by itself.

