@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@
+# ADR-010: Keep the working-tree WebVOWL baseline and restore it on failure, rather than baselining from git HEAD
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`make docs-widoco` regenerates the WIDOCO documentation and then runs
+`scripts/normalize_webvowl_json.py` over `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` to stabilize the
+nondeterministic ids and array ordering that WIDOCO's bundled OWL2VOWL step emits
+([ADR-007](007-webvowl-duplicate-node-disambiguation.md)). The normalizer needs a *baseline* —
+a previous copy of that file — so it can keep ids stable and byte-restore the previous bytes
+when nothing has changed semantically.
+
+The recipe takes that baseline from the **working-tree** copy of the file. One file therefore
+plays two roles in one recipe: it is the baseline source, copied aside before WIDOCO runs, and
+it is an rsync target, overwritten with raw generator output a few commands later. A failure
+anywhere between the rsync and a successful normalize left the working tree holding raw
+generator bytes.
+
+That by itself is a visible failure, and since 2026-08-16 the recipe runs under `set -e` so it
+reports one. The damage was done by the *next* run. It snapshotted the raw bytes as its
+baseline, generated fresh raw output, found the two semantically equal, restored the raw
+bytes — and exited 0. So a failed docs build did not stay failed: it turned into a green build
+over un-normalized output, with the only visible symptom being a whole-file reformat bundled
+into whatever real diff came next.
+
+The window is local-only. CI checks out clean, so its baseline is always the committed file,
+and the post-`make ci` drift check covers this path, so raw bytes pushed from a poisoned local
+baseline fail there.
+
+Two competing repairs were available.
+
+## Decision
+
+**Keep the working-tree baseline, and restore the pre-run bytes on failure with a shell trap.**
+
+1. `docs-widoco` records whether `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` existed before the run and,
+   if it did, keeps the copy it already makes as the restore source.
+2. A `trap … EXIT` fires on any non-zero exit: it announces itself on stderr and either copies
+   the pre-run bytes back, or removes the file if there was none before the run.
+3. The trap is disarmed (`trap - EXIT`) as soon as the normalizer succeeds. From that point the
+   file holds normalized output and every remaining command in the recipe is cleanup, so there
+   is nothing left for the trap to protect.
+
+**The rejected alternative was to take the baseline from `git show HEAD:docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json`**
+— which `scripts/normalize_webvowl_json.py` already implements as its fallback in
+`load_git_head_text`, so it would have been a smaller diff than the trap. It was rejected
+because it pays for the fix with a property the recipe is deliberately built around: a
+working-tree baseline is what makes repeated local refreshes compare against *the previous
+local run* rather than against the last commit. An author iterating on the ontology between
+commits would have every intermediate refresh compared against stale committed bytes, so the
+ids would churn against HEAD on each run instead of holding steady across the session.
+
+The deciding observation is that the two options are not symmetric in what they cost. Sourcing
+from HEAD closes the failure window by removing a wanted behaviour. The trap closes the same
+window and keeps it, and it can restore the pre-run bytes *exactly*, because the baseline copy
+the recipe already takes is a byte copy of them — the property that makes the defect possible
+is the property that makes the repair free. Ruled by Brett, 2026-09-14.
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- A failed `make docs-widoco` now leaves the tree as it found it, so re-running it after fixing
+  the cause compares against a clean baseline. A failure stays a failure.
+- No behaviour is traded away: repeated local refreshes still compare against the previous local
+  run, which is the whole reason the baseline comes from the working tree.
+- The restore is exact rather than approximate — byte-for-byte, from a copy taken in the same
+  recipe — and it is idempotent, so it is harmless when WIDOCO fails before the rsync has
+  overwritten anything.
+
+### Negative
+
+- The recipe now carries shell control flow (a trap, and one variable recording whether the file
+  pre-existed) that a reader has to follow to see why it is correct. The comment above the recipe
+  carries that reasoning, including what the trap does not cover.
+- The guard is invisible in normal operation. Nothing in a green run says the trap is armed, and
+  nothing would fail if a later edit dropped it; the only check that it still works is the
+  reproduction recorded under **More Information**, which is a manual procedure. A CI regression
+  test would need to inject a normalizer failure into a `make ci` run, which is not wired up.
+
+### Neutral
+
+- Scope is deliberately one path: `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json`. The other files `rsync`
+  copies into `docs/` are not their own baselines, so a failed run leaving them updated cannot
+  poison a later comparison, and the CI drift check catches them.
+- The normalizer's `--baseline` argument and its `load_git_head_text` fallback are untouched. The
+  fallback still applies when the recipe finds no working-tree copy to snapshot.
+
+## More Information
+
+**Reproduction, run 2026-09-14 against WIDOCO 1.4.25 / ROBOT 1.9.8, with the injected failure
+`raise ValueError` at the top of `Normalizer.normalize()`** — the same shape as the abort ADR-007
+repaired, which crashes after `rsync` has overwritten the artifact and before anything is
+written back:
+
+| step | unfixed recipe | with the trap |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1–2. break the normalizer, `make docs-widoco` | fails, `make: *** [docs-widoco] Error 1`, exit 2 | fails, `make: *** [docs-widoco] Error 1`, exit 2 |
+| 3. state of `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` | **raw WIDOCO output** (`sha256 3201d305a07959e1…`), `git status` shows ` M` | **pre-run bytes** (`sha256 baa1dc69ba550c2d…`), `git status` clean |
+| 4. repair the normalizer, re-run | **exits 0 over raw bytes** — now `567a9dcb290a0352…`, 8219 lines changed against the committed file, `git status` still ` M` | exits 0 on normalized bytes — `baa1dc69ba550c2d…`, `git status` clean |
+
+Step 1–2 is identical in both columns, which is the point: *seeing the build fail proves nothing
+about this defect*, because the unfixed recipe fails in exactly the same way. The divergence is
+at step 3, and the consequence only becomes visible at step 4, where the unfixed recipe reports
+success over output it never normalized. A clean run twice is likewise necessary and not
+sufficient — a clean run never enters the failure path at all.
+
+Step 4's unfixed hash is worth reading closely: it is neither the committed file nor the raw bytes
+from step 3, but the raw bytes of the *second* generation. The poisoned baseline was raw, the fresh
+raw output was semantically equal to it, so the normalizer restored the fresh raw ids. Every
+subsequent run does the same, so the artifact keeps churning while each build exits 0 — which is
+why this was found by inspection rather than by a failure.
+
+`baa1dc69ba550c2d…` is the committed artifact, unchanged by this ADR: no regeneration of
+`docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` is part of this decision.
+
+**What would retire the trap**: `docs-widoco` no longer overwriting the file it uses as its own
+baseline — for instance normalizing inside `release/tmp/widoco` and copying only normalized bytes
+into `docs/`, which removes the window rather than repairing it afterwards. It does **not** retire
+by adopting the rejected `git show HEAD:` baseline; that option is recorded above so it is not
+re-proposed as an improvement. The retirement condition is repeated in the Makefile comment, next
+to the trap it governs.
+
+## Related
+
+- [ADR-007](007-webvowl-duplicate-node-disambiguation.md) — the normalizer and the
+  stabilization gate this recipe wraps; its **More Information** records the `set -e` fix that
+  made this failure visible, which is the precondition for the defect this ADR closes
+- [ADR-004](004-robot-toolchain-selection.md) — the ROBOT/WIDOCO toolchain
+- `docs/tech-debt.md` — the 2026-08-16 entry that recorded this window, resolved 2026-09-14,
+  where the rejected option is also kept as history rather than deleted
+- `docs/entrypoints.md` — where the normalizer sits in the `make docs-widoco` wiring
